2:Ta83,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What foods are good for hypothyroidism in India?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For hypothyroidism, the most important dietary change is ensuring sufficient selenium, iodine, and zinc intake. In Indian meals this means including eggs regularly, using only iodised salt, eating dairy daily, including moong and masoor dal regularly for zinc, and adding a small handful of sunflower or pumpkin seeds daily. Anti-inflammatory spices like turmeric and ginger support thyroid health by reducing systemic inflammation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should hypothyroid patients avoid cabbage and cauliflower?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"This is one of the most common thyroid diet myths in India. Raw cabbage and cauliflower contain goitrogens that can theoretically interfere with iodine uptake, but cooking destroys approximately 95% of goitrogenic compounds. This means a sabzi or stir-fry of these vegetables poses virtually no thyroid risk."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does diet affect TSH levels?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Diet alone does not directly control TSH levels the same way medication does, but nutritional deficiencies significantly worsen thyroid function. Selenium deficiency impairs T4 to T3 conversion. Iodine deficiency directly reduces thyroid hormone production. Zinc deficiency impairs hormone synthesis. Addressing these through food can measurably improve thyroid function markers over 3-6 months."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I eat rice if I have thyroid issues?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, rice is generally safe for thyroid patients. Thyroid disorders are not primarily about glycaemic control. The more important consideration is ensuring sufficient thyroid-supporting micronutrients. If you have hypothyroid-associated diabetes or insulin resistance, then the diabetic guidelines for rice apply. Otherwise, 1-2 cups of cooked rice per meal within an overall balanced diet is perfectly acceptable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What Indian breakfast is best for thyroid patients?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The best thyroid breakfasts in India combine thyroid-supporting micronutrients. Egg-based breakfasts are ideal — egg bhurji or boiled eggs provide iodine, selenium, and zinc. Ragi or oats-based upma or porridge with milk provides calcium and energy. Adding sunflower seeds or pumpkin seeds to any breakfast significantly boosts selenium and zinc. The most important thyroid breakfast habit is taking levothyroxine first thing, at least 30-60 minutes before eating."}}]}0:["pBG2NdbEdegMr_aHkHTjw",[[["",{"children":["thyroid-diet-plan",{"children":["__PAGE__",{}]}]},"$undefined","$undefined",true],["",{"children":["thyroid-diet-plan",{"children":["__PAGE__",{},[["$L1",[["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"MedicalWebPage\",\"name\":\"Thyroid Diet Plan India\",\"description\":\"Personalised thyroid diet plan with selenium, iodine, and zinc-rich Indian meals for hypothyroidism support.\",\"url\":\"https://mealcoreai.com/thyroid-diet-plan\",\"about\":{\"@type\":\"MedicalCondition\",\"name\":\"Thyroid Disorder\"},\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Dr. Priya Sharma\",\"jobTitle\":\"Nutritionist\",\"url\":\"https://mealcoreai.com/about\"},\"reviewedBy\":{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Dr. Priya Sharma\",\"jobTitle\":\"Nutritionist\"},\"dateModified\":\"2026-05-02\",\"medicalAudience\":{\"@type\":\"MedicalAudience\",\"audienceType\":\"Patient\"}}"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$2"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https://mealcoreai.com\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Thyroid Diet Plan\",\"item\":\"https://mealcoreai.com/thyroid-diet-plan\"}]}"}}],["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-white","children":[["$","nav",null,{"aria-label":"Breadcrumb","className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-24 pb-0","children":["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L3",null,{"href":"/","className":"hover:text-orange-500","children":"Home"}]}],["$","li",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"/"}],["$","li",null,{"className":"text-gray-800 font-medium","children":["Thyroid"," Meal Plan"]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 sm:py-20 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-50 to-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"track-badge bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-700 border-emerald-200 mb-6 inline-flex","children":["🦋"," ","Thyroid"]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-5","children":"Thyroid Diet Plan: Indian Foods That Support Thyroid Function"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-3 mb-7 p-4 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-sm max-w-sm","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"w-10 h-10 bg-orange-100 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-lg shrink-0","aria-hidden":"true","children":"👩‍⚕️"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Dr. Priya Sharma"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs text-gray-500","children":"Nutritionist & Dietitian, MealCoreAI"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs text-green-600 font-medium","children":"✓ Reviewed for medical accuracy · April 2026"}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-2xl p-5 mb-6","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs font-bold text-blue-700 uppercase tracking-wide mb-1","children":"Quick Answer"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-800 leading-relaxed","children":"A thyroid diet plan for Indians should focus on ensuring adequate selenium, iodine, and zinc — the three minerals the thyroid needs most. Eggs, iodised salt, dairy, sunflower seeds, and moong dal are the most accessible Indian sources. Avoiding raw cruciferous vegetables is largely unnecessary since cooking destroys 95% of goitrogenic compounds."}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8","children":"The thyroid gland depends on specific micronutrients: selenium, iodine, zinc, and iron — to produce hormones effectively. Many Indians with thyroid disorders unknowingly eat diets deficient in these critical nutrients. The good news is that Indian cuisine, eaten the right way, can provide all of them. Selenium from sunflower seeds and eggs, iodine from iodised salt and dairy, and zinc from dals and pumpkin seeds are all accessible and affordable in Indian kitchens. The most common dietary concern — avoiding goitrogens (foods that can interfere with thyroid function, like raw cauliflower or unfermented soy) — is often overstated. Cooking neutralises most goitrogenic compounds. MealCoreAI's thyroid track prioritises nutrient sufficiency over unnecessary restriction, creating Indian meal plans that nourish the thyroid rather than just avoiding foods."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://mealcoreai.com/app/sign-up","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-8 py-4 rounded-full text-white font-bold shadow-lg transition-colors bg-emerald-600 hover:bg-emerald-700 shadow-emerald-200","children":["Get My ","Thyroid"," Plan Free ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round