ferences."}]]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://mealcoreai.com/app/sign-up","className":"shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-white font-semibold text-sm shadow-lg transition-colors bg-emerald-600 hover:bg-emerald-700 shadow-emerald-200","children":["Get My Personalised ","Thyroid"," Plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-4 w-4","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-gray-50","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Why Indian Food Is Ideal for ","Thyroid"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 mb-10 max-w-2xl","children":["Your kitchen is already stocked with some of the most clinically researched ingredients for ","thyroid"," management. Here's what the science says about three of them."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-10","children":[["$","div","Selenium: Brazil Nuts and Sunflower Seeds",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Selenium: Brazil Nuts and Sunflower Seeds"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Selenium is the single most important mineral for thyroid function — and most Indians don't get enough of it. The thyroid gland contains more selenium per gram than any other tissue in the body, and without it, the enzyme that converts inactive T4 into active T3 hormone simply cannot work properly. Two Brazil nuts a day is all you need. If Brazil nuts aren't accessible, a small handful of sunflower seeds daily provides a meaningful selenium dose. This is not a supplement you need — it's a food habit."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26500248/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Köhrle J, European Journal of Endocrinology, 2015."}]]}],["$","div","Iodine: Iodised Salt and Dairy",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Iodine: Iodised Salt and Dairy"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"India has one of the highest rates of iodine deficiency globally, and the thyroid literally cannot make hormones without it. Iodised salt is the simplest, cheapest, and most reliable way to ensure daily iodine intake — which is why the switch many health-conscious Indians make to pink salt, rock salt, or black salt is actually harmful for thyroid patients. These salts contain no iodine. One glass of milk provides 50-80mcg of iodine, roughly half your daily needs. Keep dairy consistent and never swap iodised salt."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9241591048","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: WHO Global Database on Iodine Deficiency, 2004."}]]}],["$","div","Zinc: Dal and Pumpkin Seeds",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Zinc: Dal and Pumpkin Seeds"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Zinc deficiency is found in a significant proportion of hypothyroid patients, and it creates a self-reinforcing problem: low thyroid function reduces zinc absorption, and low zinc reduces thyroid function. Moong and masoor dal are accessible daily sources of dietary zinc. A tablespoon of pumpkin seeds added to curd, poha, or salad provides additional zinc in a convenient form. Unlike selenium and iodine, zinc can be obtained easily through a dal-forward Indian diet — the traditional way of eating is already doing some of the work."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7932603/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Nishiyama S et al., Journal of the American College of Nutrition, 1994."}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Regional ","Thyroid"," Meal Plan Variations"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl","children":["Managing ","thyroid"," through food looks different depending on where in India you cook. Here's how it adapts across three major food traditions."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🌴 South Indian"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["South Indian cooking is built around rice, lentils, and fermented foods, all of which can be adapted for ","thyroid"," management. Swap white rice for ragi mudde or foxtail millet pongal, keep your sambar and rasam (they're excellent), and lean on pesarattu and dosas for high-protein breakfasts."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/thyroid-weekly-meal-plan-south-indian","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See South Indian ","Thyroid"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🌾 North Indian"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["The roti-dal-sabzi structure of North Indian cooking is one of the most naturally adaptable frameworks for ","thyroid",". Switch wheat atta to bajra or jowar flour, choose mustard oil or olive oil over vanaspati, and keep portions of dal generous. It's your best protein and fibre source."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/thyroid-weekly-meal-plan-north-indian","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See North Indian ","Thyroid"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🎪 Gujarati"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["Gujarati food traditions (dhokla, khichdi, thepla, handvo) are naturally portion-controlled and often dal-forward. For ","thyroid",", the traditional Gujarati thali works well with small adjustments: less jaggery in sabzis, whole grain thepla instead of maida rotla, and moong dal khichdi as a staple dinner."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/thyroid-weekly-meal-plan-gujarati","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See Gujarati ","Thyroid"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-10 bg-gray-50","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-6 flex gap-4","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-2xl shrink-0","children":"⚕️"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2","children":"When to See a Doctor"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":["Diet is one of the most powerful tools for managing ","thyroid",", but it works best alongside proper medical care. If you're newly diagnosed, experiencing severe symptoms, considering stopping medication, or your symptoms are worsening despite dietary changes, please consult your doctor or a specialist. MealCoreAI's meal plans are designed to complement medical treatment, not replace it. The nutrition guidance on this page is for educational purposes and does not constitute medical advice."]}]]}]]}]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Thyroid"," Diet: Frequently Asked Questions"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-500 mb-10","children":["Evidence-based answers to the most common questions about ","Thyroid"," nutrition."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","details","What foods are good for hypothyroidism in India?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"What foods are good for hypothyroidism in India?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"For hypothyroidism, the most important dietary change is ensuring sufficient selenium, iodine, and zinc intake. In Indian meals, this means including eggs regularly (iodine + selenium), using only iodised salt (not rock salt or pink salt), eating dairy daily (milk or curd), including moong and masoor dal regularly (zinc), and adding a small handful of sunflower or pumpkin seeds to daily meals. Anti-inflammatory spices like turmeric and ginger support thyroid health by reducing systemic inflammation that worsens hypothyroid symptoms. Vitamin D deficiency is extremely common in Indian hypothyroid patients — regular sun exposure and dairy consumption helps, but supplementation is often needed."}]]}],["$","details","Should hypothyroid patients avoid cabbage and cauliflower?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Should hypothyroid patients avoid cabbage and cauliflower?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"This is one of the most common thyroid diet myths in India. Raw cabbage and cauliflower contain goitrogens that can theoretically interfere with iodine uptake, but cooking destroys approximately 95% of goitrogenic compounds. This means a sabzi or stir-fry of these vegetables poses virtually no thyroid risk. The concern applies mainly to people who eat very large amounts of raw cruciferous vegetables daily while also being iodine deficient — an uncommon scenario for most Indians. If you are eating normal portions of cooked sabzis that include these vegetables, there is no need to avoid them. Focus your energy on ensuring adequate iodine from iodised salt and dairy instead."}]]}],["$","details","Does diet affect TSH levels?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Does diet affect TSH levels?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Diet alone does not directly control TSH levels in the same way medication does, but nutritional deficiencies significantly worsen thyroid function and can raise TSH. Selenium deficiency impairs the conversion of T4 to active T3 — the form your cells actually use. Iodine deficiency directly reduces thyroid hormone production. Zinc deficiency impairs hormone synthesis. Iron deficiency anaemia reduces thyroid hormone production efficiency. Addressing these deficiencies through food can measurably improve thyroid function markers over 3-6 months and may allow medication doses to be optimised. However, dietary changes should always complement — never replace — prescribed thyroid medication."}]]}],["$","details","Can I eat rice if I have thyroid issues?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Can I eat rice if I have thyroid issues?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Yes, rice is generally safe for thyroid patients. Thyroid disorders are not primarily about glycaemic control the way diabetes is, so the type of grain matters less than ensuring you get sufficient thyroid-supporting micronutrients. The more important consideration is that people with hypothyroidism often have a slower metabolism and may gain weight easily, making portion control of all refined carbs including white rice sensible. Choosing brown rice over white adds fibre and more micronutrients. If you have hypothyroid-associated diabetes or insulin resistance, then following the diabetic guidelines for rice applies. Otherwise, 1-2 cups of cooked rice per meal within an overall balanced diet is perfectly acceptable."}]]}],["$","details","What Indian breakfast is best for thyroid patients?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"What Indian breakfast is best for thyroid patients?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"The best thyroid breakfasts in India combine thyroid-supporting micronutrients with satisfying nutrition. Egg-based breakfasts are ideal — egg bhurji or boiled eggs provide iodine, selenium, and zinc in one shot. Ragi or oats-based upma or porridge with milk provides calcium and energy. Including a glass of milk at breakfast gives consistent iodine intake. For vegetarians, a breakfast of idli-sambar with curd on the side provides protein and iodine. Adding sunflower seeds or pumpkin seeds to any breakfast significantly boosts selenium and zinc. The most important thyroid breakfast habit is taking your levothyroxine or thyroid medication first thing in the morning, at least 30-60 minutes before eating or drinking anything except water."}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-14 bg-orange-50/60","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-7","children":"More Thyroid Resources"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4","children":[["$","$L3","/meal-plans/thyroid-weekly-meal-plan-north-indian",{"href":"/meal-plans/thyroid-weekly-meal-plan-north-indian","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"North Indian thyroid-friendly weekly meal plan"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children":["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3.5 w-3.5","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]}]]}],["$","$L3","/blog/thyroid-foods-avoid-india",{"href":"/blog/thyroid-foods-avoid-india","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"Foods to avoid with thyroid — Indian diet guide"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children":["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3.5 w-3.5","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]}]]}],["$","$L3","/blog/thyroid-weight-gain-diet",{"href":"/blog/thyroid-weight-gain-diet","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"Managing thyroid weight gain with an Indian diet"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children"